course: biology
lesson: 380
title: China's plants: bamboo, ginkgo, lotus, tea and rice paddies
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Bamboo is a giant grass, and some kinds grow close to a metre in a single day: if you sat beside a shoot with a ruler, you could almost watch it stretch. Its hollow stems with solid joints are strong and light, so Hong Kong builders still tie bamboo poles into scaffolding round skyscrapers.
explain: The ginkgo tree with its fan-shaped leaves is a living fossil whose family goes back about 200 million years, to dinosaur times. It is so tough that ginkgos near the Hiroshima bomb sprouted again from their roots, kept safe under the soil.
explain: Pour water on a lotus leaf and it rolls off in silver balls, carrying the dirt with it, because the leaf is covered in tiny waxy bumps. A lotus seed about 1,300 years old was even grown into a plant.
explain: All real tea, green or black, comes from one plant, and rice grows in flooded fields that drown weeds. On mountains in Yunnan people carved rice terraces like giant steps, and in Zhejiang farmers keep fish among the rice.
fact: Bamboo is a giant grass; some kinds grow close to a metre in a day, and a shoot comes out as thick as it will ever be and just stretches.
fact: Bamboo stems are hollow with solid joints, which makes them strong and light, and bamboo bends without breaking and grows back quickly.
fact: Some bamboos flower only once in about 60 to 120 years, all plants of one kind flowering at the same time and then dying; in the 1970s and 1980s mass flowering in China left giant pandas short of food.
fact: Builders in Hong Kong still use bamboo scaffolding tied with plastic strips, though more and more metal scaffolding is used there too.
fact: The ginkgo is a living fossil, its family going back about 200 million years, and it has no close living relatives; its name biloba means two lobes, after the split leaf.
fact: Several ginkgo trees about one to two kilometres from the Hiroshima atomic bomb sprouted again from their roots and are still alive and protected as symbols of hope.
fact: Ginkgos are tough against pollution, so many cities plant them on streets.
fact: Ginkgo seeds have a fleshy coat that smells like sick or rancid butter because of an acid in it; only female trees make seeds, so many cities plant only male trees, and the inside nut is cooked and eaten in China in small amounts.
fact: A lotus leaf has tiny bumps covered in wax, so water rolls off taking dirt with it; this lotus effect was copied for self-cleaning paint and glass, and in Buddhism the lotus stands for something pure rising from mud.
fact: A lotus seed about 1,300 years old from a dry lake bed in China was grown into a plant, thanks to its hard waterproof seed coat, and lotus flowers can warm themselves to about 30 to 35 degrees to attract insects.
fact: All real tea, green, black, white and oolong, comes from one plant, Camellia sinensis; the difference is how the leaves are dried, rolled and left to darken in air.
fact: Tea pickers pick just the bud and the top two leaves, and a Chinese legend says Emperor Shennong found tea when a leaf fell into his hot water.
fact: Rice can grow in flooded fields because its stems have air channels carrying air to the roots, and the water drowns many weeds; the Hani people in Yunnan built rice terraces up whole mountains over about 1,300 years.
fact: In Zhejiang farmers have kept fish in their rice fields for over 1,200 years; the fish eat insects and weeds and their droppings feed the rice, and it is protected as a world farming heritage.
exercise: What kind of plant is bamboo? | A giant grass | A kind of tree like oak | A kind of fern | A kind of cactus
exercise: How fast can some bamboo grow? | Close to a metre in a single day | About a metre in ten years | A centimetre a year | It does not grow at all after it is first planted
exercise: Why is a bamboo stem strong but light? | It is hollow with solid joints | It is filled with metal from the soil | It is solid stone inside | It is full of water
exercise: How often do some bamboos flower? | Only once in about 60 to 120 years | Every spring, like apple trees | Every week | Never at all, it only grows from bits
exercise: What happens to bamboo plants after they all flower at once? | They die, and grow back slowly from seed | They double in size | They turn into trees | They flower again next week and then every week after
exercise: Why were giant pandas short of food in the 1970s and 1980s? | Mass flowering made their bamboo die | Pandas ate too much meat | Bamboo was cut down for scaffolding | Snow covered all the bamboo for ten years
exercise: Why is bamboo good for scaffolding? | It is strong, light and bends without breaking | It is very heavy and stiff, so it never moves | It never needs tying | It glows in the dark
exercise: Why is the ginkgo called a living fossil? | Its family goes back about 200 million years with no close relatives | It is made of stone | It grows only in museums | It was found frozen in ice and woke up again when the ice melted
exercise: What does biloba, the ginkgo's name, mean? | Two lobes | Golden leaf | Old tree | Bad smell
exercise: How did ginkgos near Hiroshima survive the atomic bomb? | They sprouted again from their roots | They were inside a strong concrete shelter | They were planted afterwards | They had metal bark
exercise: Why do many cities plant only male ginkgo trees? | Only females make seeds that smell like sick | Male trees grow faster | Female trees are poisonous to touch, even their leaves | Male trees have bigger leaves that give more shade
exercise: Why does water roll off a lotus leaf in balls? | Tiny waxy bumps stop it from spreading | The leaf is hot and boils it | The leaf drinks it at once | The leaf is made of glass
exercise: What did engineers copy from the lotus leaf? | Self-cleaning paint and glass | Waterproof shoes made of leaves | Paper made from petals | Glue for wallpaper
exercise: How old was the lotus seed that was grown into a plant? | About 1,300 years | About 13 years | About 13,000,000 years | About 1 year
exercise: Where do green tea and black tea come from? | The same plant, Camellia sinensis | Two different trees | Green tea from grass and black from coffee | Black tea from burnt leaves of any plant
exercise: What do tea pickers pick from each shoot? | The bud and the top two leaves | The roots | Only the oldest leaves at the bottom | The flowers and seeds
exercise: Why can rice live in flooded fields? | Its stems have air channels carrying air to the roots | It breathes underwater like a fish, through gills | It does not need air | It floats on top of the water like a lily pad with its roots hanging
exercise: Why might a farmer who keeps fish in rice fields need less pest spray? | The fish eat insects and weeds | Fish scare away the farmer | Fish make the rice taste bad to bugs | Fish water the plants
test: Count them: a tea picker takes one bud and the top two leaves from each of 5 shoots. How many pieces? | 15 | 10 | 5 | 20
test: Count them: a ginkgo leaf has 2 lobes. How many lobes on 7 leaves? | 14 | 9 | 7 | 21
test: Count them: a bamboo shoot grows about 90 centimetres a day. About how much in 2 days? | 180 centimetres | 90 centimetres | 45 centimetres | 900 centimetres
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Po the panda lives on noodles and dumplings. What do real giant pandas mostly eat? | Bamboo | Noodles and dumplings | Fish and rice | Honey and berries
test: «green tea n black tea, totally different plants, obviously» What is right? | Both come from one plant, only the drying is different | Yes, black tea comes from a black tree | Green tea is made from grass | Black tea is burnt green tea leaves from any plant
test: «that tree under the window stinks like sick, someone dropped something» What is a likely cause? | Squashed seeds from a female ginkgo tree | A broken water pipe | A sick fox | The leaves are rotting in spring because the tree is dying
test: Why might a bendy pole be safer than a stiff one in strong winds? | It bends and springs back instead of snapping | It is lighter so the wind cannot touch it | Stiff poles are always made of glass | Bendy poles stop the wind
test: What would happen to most other crops in a flooded rice field? | Their roots would get no air and they would die | They would grow faster than rice | They would turn into rice | They would float away and grow again further down the river
test: Why would flowering all at once help bamboo seeds survive hungry seed-eaters? | There are too many seeds for the animals to eat them all | Seed-eaters sleep that year | The seeds are poisonous only then | The animals do not see bamboo flowers, because they are green like the leaves
test: «lotus seed growing after 1,300 years, that's fake news» What is right? | It really happened; its hard waterproof coat kept it alive | Seeds die after one year | It was a new seed painted to look old by scientists who wanted to be famous | Lotus seeds are stones
test: What could you make if you could copy a lotus leaf? | Windows and paint that clean themselves in the rain | Leaves that turn into gold | Paper that never tears, even when wet | Cups that keep water hot all day and all night
test: Why might roots survive when everything above ground burns? | The soil shields them from the heat | Roots are made of stone | Fire cannot reach the dark | Roots grow back faster than flames
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous cities plant exclusively male ginkgo trees. | Only female trees bear seeds whose fleshy coat emits an offensive odour | Male trees attain greater height | Female trees are toxic to the touch | Male trees are more tolerant of drought and of the pollution of city streets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning green tea and black tea. | Both are derived from one species, Camellia sinensis, and differ in processing | They are derived from two unrelated species | Black tea is obtained from coffee plants | Green tea is a variety of grass
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity of rice to grow in flooded fields? | Its stems contain air channels which convey air to the roots | It extracts oxygen from water by means of gills | It requires no air whatsoever | It floats upon the surface of the water, its roots suspended beneath
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a stem of bamboo combines strength with lightness. | It is hollow and divided at intervals by solid joints | It is filled throughout with dense fibre | It is reinforced by minerals of iron which it draws from the soil | It contains a reservoir of water
